--- /dev/null
+++ b/docs/deployment.md
@@ -0,0 +1,90 @@
+# Deploying Fizzy with Kamal
+
+The default `config/deploy.yml` runs self-hosted Fizzy on **one Linux server**. One application container runs Rails, SQLite, local file storage, and Solid Queue background jobs through Puma. Kamal Proxy runs alongside it, terminates HTTPS, and obtains and renews Let's Encrypt certificates. No MySQL, Redis, S3, or separate job server is required.
+
+This setup is independent of the private SaaS configuration under `saas/`. Run the commands below from the root of your fork, without SaaS mode enabled (`SAAS` or `tmp/saas.txt`).
+
+## Prerequisites
+
+- A Linux server with Docker, persistent disk space, and SSH key access. Kamal connects as `root` by default; to use another user, add `ssh: { user: your-user }` to the deploy config and give that user permission to run Docker.
+- A hostname whose DNS A record (and AAAA record, if present) points directly at the server. Ports **80 and 443** must be publicly reachable for HTTPS and certificate issuance, and SSH must be reachable from your deployment machine. Do not put another proxy in front of the server while setting this up.
+- An SMTP service and a verified sender address. Fizzy uses email verification codes for sign-in, so working outbound mail is required.
+- A Docker Hub account/repository and an access token with permission to push and pull your image. You can use another registry by setting `registry.server` and adjusting `image` and the credentials.
+- On your local deployment machine: Git, Docker with Buildx, the Ruby version in `.ruby-version`, and the gems installed with `bundle install`. `bin/kamal` uses the version bundled with Fizzy (equivalent to `bundle exec kamal`).
+
+## Configure your fork
+
+1. Edit `config/deploy.yml`:
+   - Replace `fizzy.example.com` with your hostname, without `https://` or a path. The YAML alias reuses this value for the SSH host, proxy, and `APP_HOST` used in generated links.
+   - Replace `your-dockerhub-user` in both `image` and `registry.username`.
+   - Set `MAILER_FROM_ADDRESS`, `SMTP_ADDRESS`, `SMTP_PORT`, `SMTP_DOMAIN` (your SMTP HELO domain), and `SMTP_AUTHENTICATION` to match your mail provider. The defaults use port 587 with STARTTLS. For implicit TLS on port 465, set `SMTP_TLS: "true"` and `SMTP_ENABLE_STARTTLS_AUTO: "false"`. For an unauthenticated relay, set `SMTP_AUTHENTICATION: ""` and remove `SMTP_USERNAME` and `SMTP_PASSWORD` from `env.secret`.
+   - The builder targets `amd64`; change `builder.arch` to `arm64` if that matches your server. Cross-architecture builds require Docker's emulation support.
+   - The web and job worker counts start at one each. Increase `WEB_CONCURRENCY` and `JOB_CONCURRENCY` only as memory and workload allow.
+
+2. Create your private secrets file:
+
+   ```sh
+   cp .kamal/secrets.example .kamal/secrets
+   chmod 600 .kamal/secrets
+   bin/rails secret
+   ```
+
+   Fill in `.kamal/secrets` with your registry token, the generated `SECRET_KEY_BASE`, and SMTP username/password. Quote values containing spaces or special characters using dotenv syntax. You can instead reference exported environment variables as shown in the template.
+
+   **Keep `SECRET_KEY_BASE` stable across deploys** and store it securely with your backups. No `RAILS_MASTER_KEY` or private 37signals credentials are required for this setup. Never commit `.kamal/secrets`; it is ignored by Git and excluded from the Docker build context.
+
+3. Commit your non-secret configuration changes. Kamal builds from the Git checkout by default, so uncommitted application or Dockerfile changes will not be included in the image.
+
+## First deployment
+
+```sh
+bin/kamal setup
+```
+
+Kamal builds and pushes your image, starts the proxy, and boots Fizzy. The Docker entrypoint runs `db:prepare` to create or migrate **all four SQLite databases** before starting the server. Puma starts Solid Queue workers and recurring jobs in the same application container.
+
+Visit `https://your-hostname` and create your account. Verify that your sign-in email arrives. If deployment or mail delivery fails, inspect the logs:
+
+```sh
+bin/kamal logs
+```
+
+Keep Rails' SSL defaults enabled: Kamal Proxy terminates TLS, while the application receives HTTP on port 80 through Thruster. There is no need to set `DISABLE_SSL`, expose Puma directly, or configure a certificate inside the application container.
+
+## Subsequent deploys and maintenance
+
+Commit your changes, then deploy with:
+
+```sh
+bin/kamal deploy
+```
+
+Useful commands:
+
+```sh
+bin/kamal console
+bin/kamal shell
+bin/kamal dbconsole
+bin/kamal app details
+```
+
+The named Docker volume **`fizzy_storage`**, mounted at **`/rails/storage`**, holds:
+
+- `production.sqlite3`: application data
+- `production_queue.sqlite3`: background jobs and recurring task state
+- `production_cache.sqlite3` and `production_cable.sqlite3`: cache and realtime messaging
+- `production/files`: uploaded attachments and other locally stored files
+
+The volume is reused by new application containers, so data survives container replacement and normal deploys. Do not rename the volume or move Fizzy to another host without migrating this data. Do not delete the volume or prune it while the application is stopped. This configuration is for a single host, not a horizontally scaled SQLite deployment.
+
+**Persistence is not a backup.** Arrange off-server backups of the entire volume and your secrets. For a simple consistent backup, stop the app (`bin/kamal app stop`), snapshot or copy the whole volume including any SQLite WAL files, then start it again (`bin/kamal app start`). Stopping the application also stops background jobs. Test restoration before relying on your backups.
+
+## Optional browser push notifications
+
+Email and the rest of Fizzy work without VAPID keys. To enable browser push notifications, generate a key pair:
+
+```sh
+bundle exec ruby -rweb-push -e 'key = WebPush.generate_key; puts "VAPID_PUBLIC_KEY=#{key.public_key}", "VAPID_PRIVATE_KEY=#{key.private_key}"'
+```
+
+Add the keys to `.kamal/secrets`, uncomment their entries in `env.secret` in `config/deploy.yml`, and deploy. Keep the pair stable across deploys so existing subscriptions continue to work.
diff --git a/test/lib/production_configuration_test.rb b/test/lib/production_configuration_test.rb
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..044c73e
--- /dev/null
+++ b/test/lib/production_configuration_test.rb
@@ -0,0 +1,105 @@
+require "test_helper"
+require "open3"
+
+class ProductionConfigurationTest < ActiveSupport::TestCase
+  test "self-hosted mail and background jobs use the public HTTPS hostname" do
+    config = production_configuration(
+      "APP_HOST" => "fizzy.example.com",
+      "SMTP_ADDRESS" => "smtp.example.com",
+      "SMTP_USERNAME" => "smtp-user",
+      "SMTP_PASSWORD" => "smtp-password"
+    )
+
+    assert_equal "smtp.example.com", config.dig("smtp", "address")
+    assert_equal 587, config.dig("smtp", "port")
+    assert_equal "fizzy.example.com", config.dig("smtp", "domain")
+    assert_equal "smtp-user", config.dig("smtp", "user_name")
+    assert_equal "smtp-password", config.dig("smtp", "password")
+    assert_equal "plain", config.dig("smtp", "authentication")
+    assert_equal true, config.dig("smtp", "enable_starttls_auto")
+    assert_equal false, config.dig("smtp", "tls")
+    assert_equal({ "host" => "fizzy.example.com", "protocol" => "https" }, config["mailer_urls"])
+    assert_equal config["mailer_urls"], config["route_urls"]
+    assert_equal "https://fizzy.example.com/up", config["health_url"]
+    assert_equal true, config["force_ssl"]
+    assert_equal true, config["assume_ssl"]
+  end
+
+  test "SMTP supports implicit TLS and provider-specific settings" do
+    config = production_configuration(
+      "SMTP_ADDRESS" => "smtp.example.com",
+      "SMTP_PORT" => "465",
+      "SMTP_DOMAIN" => "mail.example.com",
+      "SMTP_AUTHENTICATION" => "login",
+      "SMTP_TLS" => "true",
+      "SMTP_ENABLE_STARTTLS_AUTO" => "false"
+    )
+
+    assert_equal 465, config.dig("smtp", "port")
+    assert_equal "mail.example.com", config.dig("smtp", "domain")
+    assert_equal "login", config.dig("smtp", "authentication")
+    assert_equal true, config.dig("smtp", "tls")
+    assert_equal false, config.dig("smtp", "enable_starttls_auto")
+  end
+
+  test "SMTP supports an unauthenticated relay" do
+    config = production_configuration(
+      "SMTP_ADDRESS" => "relay.example.com",
+      "SMTP_PORT" => "25",
+      "SMTP_AUTHENTICATION" => ""
+    )
+
+    assert_equal 25, config.dig("smtp", "port")
+    assert_nil config.dig("smtp", "authentication")
+    assert_nil config.dig("smtp", "user_name")
+    assert_nil config.dig("smtp", "password")
+  end
+
+  test "mail defaults are unchanged without self-hosting environment variables" do
+    config = production_configuration
+
+    assert_equal "localhost", config.dig("smtp", "address")
+    assert_equal 25, config.dig("smtp", "port")
+    assert_empty config["mailer_urls"]
+    assert_empty config["route_urls"]
+  end
+
+  test "public URLs respect the existing SSL opt-out" do
+    config = production_configuration("APP_HOST" => "fizzy.example.com", "DISABLE_SSL" => "true")
+
+    assert_equal "http", config.dig("mailer_urls", "protocol")
+    assert_equal "http://fizzy.example.com/up", config["health_url"]
+    assert_equal false, config["force_ssl"]
+    assert_equal false, config["assume_ssl"]
+  end
+
+  private
+    def production_configuration(overrides = {})
+      environment = %w[
+        APP_HOST SMTP_ADDRESS SMTP_PORT SMTP_DOMAIN SMTP_USERNAME SMTP_PASSWORD
+        SMTP_AUTHENTICATION SMTP_TLS SMTP_ENABLE_STARTTLS_AUTO DISABLE_SSL FORCE_SSL ASSUME_SSL
+      ].index_with { nil }.merge(
+        "RAILS_ENV" => "production",
+        "SAAS" => "false",
+        "DATABASE_ADAPTER" => "sqlite",
+        "ACTIVE_STORAGE_SERVICE" => "local",
+        "SECRET_KEY_BASE" => "test-secret-key-base-" + "a" * 128
+      ).merge(overrides)
+
+      # Environment configuration must be tested in a fresh production process, not
+      # by reloading it into the already initialized test application.
+      output, error, status = Open3.capture3(environment, "bin/rails", "runner", <<~'CODE', chdir: Rails.root)
+        puts JSON.generate(
+          smtp: ActionMailer::Base.smtp_settings,
+          mailer_urls: ActionMailer::Base.default_url_options,
+          route_urls: Rails.application.routes.default_url_options,
+          health_url: ENV["APP_HOST"] && Rails.application.routes.url_helpers.rails_health_check_url,
+          force_ssl: Rails.application.config.force_ssl,
+          assume_ssl: Rails.application.config.assume_ssl
+        )
+      CODE
+
+      assert status.success?, "Production boot failed:\n#{output}\n#{error}"
+      JSON.parse(output.lines.last)
+    end
+end
